<section class="hero searchable" id="top">
  <div class="eyebrow">Primary hyperparathyroidism</div>
  <h1>Patient-centered clinical workflow</h1>
  <p class="lead">Built to answer the questions that matter in clinic: Is the calcium truly high? Is the process PTH-dependent? Is this PHPT or a mimic? What complications must be assessed? Does the patient need surgery? What is the plan if surgery is deferred?</p>
  <div class="meta"><span class="pill">Interactive lab interpretation</span><span class="pill">Differential diagnosis</span><span class="pill">Current surgical thresholds</span><span class="pill">Postoperative follow-up</span></div>
</section>

<section id="start" class="searchable">
<h2>Start here: the six decisions</h2>
<div class="grid3">
  <div class="card"><div class="label">1</div><div class="question">Is calcium truly elevated?</div><p class="muted">Repeat it, account for albumin, and obtain ionized calcium when the total calcium is unreliable or discordant.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label">2</div><div class="question">Is PTH suppressed?</div><p class="muted">This is the main fork. Nonsuppressed PTH means PTH-dependent hypercalcemia. Suppressed PTH sends the workup elsewhere.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label">3</div><div class="question">Is it PHPT or a mimic?</div><p class="muted">Exclude FHH, lithium/thiazide effects, tertiary hyperparathyroidism, and misleading urine-calcium results.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label teal">4</div><div class="question">Is there organ damage?</div><p class="muted">Look for silent stones, nephrocalcinosis, reduced renal function, osteoporosis, and vertebral fracture.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label teal">5</div><div class="question">Does the patient meet surgical criteria?</div><p class="muted">Only one criterion is needed. Surgery can still be chosen without a formal criterion if appropriate.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label teal">6</div><div class="question">What happens next?</div><p class="muted">Choose surgery, targeted medical therapy, surveillance, or urgent stabilization based on the actual problem.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="callout red"><strong>Do not start with a sestamibi scan.</strong> PHPT is a biochemical diagnosis. Localization imaging is ordered only after the patient is going to surgery.</div>
</section>

<section id="interpreter" class="searchable">
<h2>Interactive case interpreter</h2>
<p>Enter the values you have. The output structures the reasoning; it does not replace review of the chart, assay ranges, medications, collection quality, or specialist judgment.</p>
<div class="formcard">
  <h3 style="margin-top:0">Patient and serum studies</h3>
  <div class="formgrid">
    <div class="field"><label for="age">Age</label><input id="age" type="number" min="0" placeholder="e.g., 58"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="sex">Sex used for urine-Ca threshold</label><select id="sex"><option value="">Select</option><option value="female">Female</option><option value="male">Male</option></select></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="ca">Total calcium (mg/dL)</label><input id="ca" type="number" step="0.01" placeholder="e.g., 10.8"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="cauln">Calcium ULN (mg/dL)</label><input id="cauln" type="number" step="0.01" value="10.2"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="albumin">Albumin (g/dL)</label><input id="albumin" type="number" step="0.01" placeholder="e.g., 3.4"><small>Correction is only an estimate.</small></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="ionca">Ionized calcium</label><input id="ionca" type="number" step="0.001" placeholder="optional"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="ioncauln">Ionized Ca ULN</label><input id="ioncauln" type="number" step="0.001" placeholder="lab-specific"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="pth">Intact PTH (pg/mL)</label><input id="pth" type="number" step="0.1" placeholder="e.g., 61"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="pthlln">PTH LLN</label><input id="pthlln" type="number" step="0.1" value="15"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="pthuln">PTH ULN</label><input id="pthuln" type="number" step="0.1" value="65"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="vitd">25-OH vitamin D (ng/mL)</label><input id="vitd" type="number" step="0.1" placeholder="e.g., 24"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="egfr">eGFR or CrCl (mL/min)</label><input id="egfr" type="number" step="0.1" placeholder="e.g., 74"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="phos">Phosphorus (mg/dL)</label><input id="phos" type="number" step="0.01" placeholder="optional"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="alp">Alkaline phosphatase</label><input id="alp" type="number" step="0.1" placeholder="optional"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="tScore">Lowest T-score</label><input id="tScore" type="number" step="0.1" placeholder="e.g., -2.7"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="caHistory">Prior calcium pattern</label><select id="caHistory"><option value="unknown">Unknown</option><option value="new">New/progressive</option><option value="lifelong">Lifelong/stable</option></select></div>
  </div>

  <h3>24-hour urine data</h3>
  <div class="formgrid">
    <div class="field"><label for="urineCa">Urine calcium (mg/24 h)</label><input id="urineCa" type="number" step="0.1" placeholder="e.g., 180"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="urineCr">Urine creatinine (mg/24 h)</label><input id="urineCr" type="number" step="0.1" placeholder="e.g., 1300"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="serumCr">Serum creatinine (mg/dL)</label><input id="serumCr" type="number" step="0.01" placeholder="e.g., 0.9"></div>
    <div class="field"><label for="collection">Collection quality</label><select id="collection"><option value="unknown">Unknown</option><option value="adequate">Appears adequate</option><option value="questionable">Questionable/incomplete</option></select></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Clinical context and confounders</h3>
  <div class="checkgrid">
    <label class="check"><input id="stone" type="checkbox"> Kidney stone or nephrocalcinosis</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="vertebral" type="checkbox"> Vertebral or low-trauma fracture</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="thiazide" type="checkbox"> Thiazide use</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="lithium" type="checkbox"> Lithium use</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="biotin" type="checkbox"> High-dose biotin exposure</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="family" type="checkbox"> Family history of hypercalcemia</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="malabsorption" type="checkbox"> Bariatric surgery or malabsorption</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="lowCaIntake" type="checkbox"> Low calcium intake</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="antiresorptive" type="checkbox"> Bisphosphonate or denosumab use</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="dialysis" type="checkbox"> Long-standing CKD/dialysis or renal transplant</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="symptomatic" type="checkbox"> Overt PHPT complication/symptomatic disease</label>
    <label class="check"><input id="pregnant" type="checkbox"> Pregnancy</label>
  </div>
  <div class="actions"><button class="btn" id="interpretBtn">Interpret this case</button><button class="btn secondary" id="resetBtn">Reset</button></div>

  <div id="caseResult" class="result" aria-live="polite">
    <div class="resultgrid">
      <div class="resultbox"><h4>1. Calcium status</h4><div id="calciumOutput"></div></div>
      <div class="resultbox"><h4>2. PTH branch</h4><div id="pthOutput"></div></div>
      <div class="resultbox"><h4>3. Leading diagnostic direction</h4><div id="diagnosisOutput"></div></div>
      <div class="resultbox"><h4>4. Urine calcium / FHH assessment</h4><div id="urineOutput"></div></div>
      <div class="resultbox"><h4>5. Surgical criteria</h4><div id="surgeryOutput"></div></div>
      <div class="resultbox"><h4>6. Next actions</h4><div id="nextOutput"></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Corrected calcium limitation:</strong> the common formula is a rough estimate and can be misleading in critical illness, major protein abnormalities, acid-base disturbance, or marked hypoalbuminemia. Use ionized calcium when the result will change the diagnosis.</div>
</section>

<section id="orders" class="searchable">
<h2>What to order</h2>
<div class="grid2">
  <div class="card"><div class="label">Initial abnormal calcium</div><ul class="tight"><li>Repeat total calcium and albumin</li><li>Intact PTH</li><li>Creatinine/eGFR</li><li>Phosphorus</li><li>25-OH vitamin D</li><li>Ionized calcium if total calcium is uncertain or discordant</li><li>Medication/supplement review: calcium, vitamin D, thiazide, lithium, biotin, vitamin A</li></ul></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label teal">After PTH-dependent hypercalcemia is confirmed</div><ul class="tight"><li>Full 24-hour urine calcium and creatinine</li><li>DXA: lumbar spine, total hip/femoral neck, distal one-third radius</li><li>Vertebral fracture assessment or vertebral radiographs when indicated</li><li>Renal imaging for nephrolithiasis/nephrocalcinosis</li><li>Stone-risk profile if stones or hypercalciuria</li><li>Genetic evaluation when age/family/multigland pattern warrants it</li></ul></div>
</div>
<h3>Build a copyable order set</h3>
<div class="orderlist" id="orderList">
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="Repeat total calcium and albumin"> Repeat total calcium + albumin</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="Ionized calcium"> Ionized calcium</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="Intact PTH"> Intact PTH</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="25-hydroxyvitamin D"> 25-OH vitamin D</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="Phosphorus, magnesium, alkaline phosphatase"> Phosphorus, magnesium, ALP</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="Creatinine with eGFR; calculate or measure creatinine clearance when needed"> Creatinine/eGFR or CrCl</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="24-hour urine calcium and creatinine"> 24-hour urine calcium + creatinine</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="Renal imaging for nephrolithiasis/nephrocalcinosis"> Renal imaging</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="DXA: lumbar spine, total hip/femoral neck, distal one-third radius"> Three-site DXA</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="Vertebral fracture assessment or vertebral radiographs"> VFA/vertebral imaging</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="Biochemical stone-risk profile"> Stone-risk profile</label>
  <label class="orderitem"><input type="checkbox" data-order="Genetic counseling/testing based on age, family history, multigland disease, or syndromic features"> Genetic evaluation</label>
</div>
<div class="actions"><button class="btn teal" id="buildOrders">Build order text</button><button class="btn secondary" id="copyOrders">Copy</button></div>
<div id="ordersText" class="copybox">Select orders above, then click “Build order text.”</div>
</section>

<section id="note" class="searchable">
<h2>Assessment & plan builder</h2>
<p>A useful endocrine note should show your reasoning, not just state “hyperparathyroidism.”</p>
<div class="card">
  <ol>
    <li><strong>State diagnostic status:</strong> suspected, biochemically confirmed, or not yet established.</li>
    <li><strong>Show the biochemical evidence:</strong> calcium pattern, albumin/ionized calcium, PTH response, repeat dates.</li>
    <li><strong>Address mimics and confounders:</strong> FHH, medications, CKD, vitamin D, malabsorption, collection quality.</li>
    <li><strong>Document organ evaluation:</strong> renal imaging, 24-hour urine, renal function, three-site DXA, vertebral assessment.</li>
    <li><strong>State whether surgery criteria are met.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Give a concrete next step:</strong> repeat labs, complete workup, surgeon referral, medication, or surveillance interval.</li>
  </ol>
</div>
<div class="copybox" id="noteTemplate">Assessment:
- PTH-dependent hypercalcemia / elevated PTH with normal calcium: [status].
- Biochemical evidence: calcium [ ], albumin [ ], ionized calcium [ ], PTH [ ] on [dates].
- Confounders/mimics reviewed: [vitamin D, kidney function, thiazide/lithium, biotin, calcium intake, malabsorption, FHH].
- 24-hour urine calcium [ ] mg/day; collection creatinine [ ]; CCCR [ ] and limitations discussed.
- Target-organ assessment: DXA [ ]; vertebral assessment [ ]; renal imaging [ ]; eGFR/CrCl [ ].
- Surgical criteria: [met/not met], based on [criterion].

Plan:
1. [Confirm/complete biochemical workup].
2. [Complete skeletal and renal evaluation].
3. [Refer to experienced parathyroid surgeon / monitor / use targeted medical therapy].
4. Maintain normal calcium intake, hydration, and 25-OH vitamin D >30 ng/mL with monitoring.
5. Follow-up: [interval and labs].</div>
<div class="actions"><button class="btn secondary" onclick="copyText('noteTemplate')">Copy note skeleton</button></div>
</section>

<section id="algorithm" class="searchable">
<h2>Diagnostic algorithm</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="step"><strong>Confirm the calcium abnormality.</strong> Repeat total calcium with albumin. Use ionized calcium when albumin correction is unreliable, total and clinical picture conflict, or normocalcemic PHPT is being considered.</div>
  <div class="step"><strong>Interpret PTH against the calcium—not against the reference range alone.</strong> In true hypercalcemia, PTH should be suppressed. A mid-normal or high-normal PTH is physiologically abnormal.</div>
  <div class="step"><strong>If PTH is suppressed:</strong> stop calling this PHPT. Evaluate PTH-independent hypercalcemia based on the clinical context.</div>
  <div class="step"><strong>If PTH is elevated or nonsuppressed:</strong> consider PHPT first, then actively assess FHH, thiazide/lithium exposure, and tertiary HPT.</div>
  <div class="step"><strong>Confirm persistence.</strong> Hypercalcemic PHPT requires elevated albumin-adjusted calcium with elevated or inappropriately normal PTH on two occasions at least two weeks apart.</div>
  <div class="step"><strong>Complete renal and skeletal staging.</strong> The patient may appear asymptomatic yet have silent stones, nephrocalcinosis, osteoporosis, or vertebral fracture.</div>
  <div class="step"><strong>Decide on surgery before localization.</strong> Once surgery is chosen, obtain localization to plan the operation.</div>
</div>
<h3>The main PTH fork</h3>
<div class="grid2">
  <div class="branch"><span class="status red">PTH suppressed</span><h4>Non-PTH-mediated hypercalcemia</h4><p>Think malignancy/PTHrP, vitamin D excess, granulomatous disease or lymphoma, medications, thyrotoxicosis, adrenal insufficiency, immobilization, milk-alkali physiology, and other context-specific causes.</p></div>
  <div class="branch"><span class="status blue">PTH nonsuppressed</span><h4>PTH-dependent hypercalcemia</h4><p>PHPT is most common. Differentiate FHH, lithium/thiazide-associated physiology, tertiary HPT, and very rare ectopic PTH secretion.</p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="labs" class="searchable">
<h2>Interpret every laboratory result</h2>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Why order it</th><th>What supports PHPT</th><th>Common trap</th><th>What to do next</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Total calcium + albumin</strong></td><td>Confirm hypercalcemia and estimate adjusted calcium.</td><td>Repeated adjusted calcium above the laboratory ULN.</td><td>Correction formulas are imprecise; dehydration and albumin shifts can mislead.</td><td>Repeat. Obtain ionized calcium if the diagnosis remains uncertain.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Ionized calcium</strong></td><td>Directly measures biologically active calcium.</td><td>Elevated in hypercalcemic PHPT; must be normal for normocalcemic PHPT.</td><td>Specimen handling and pH affect the result.</td><td>Use when total calcium is discordant or normocalcemic PHPT is being considered.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Intact PTH</strong></td><td>Separates PTH-dependent from PTH-independent hypercalcemia.</td><td>Elevated or inappropriately normal during hypercalcemia.</td><td>Reading “normal PTH” as reassuring. Biotin may produce falsely low results. CKD can raise measured PTH.</td><td>Interpret with calcium, kidney function, assay range, and supplements.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Phosphorus</strong></td><td>Supports PTH physiology and flags CKD/phosphate disorders.</td><td>Often low or low-normal from phosphaturia.</td><td>Normal phosphorus does not exclude PHPT.</td><td>Use context; high phosphorus with high PTH and CKD points away from simple sporadic PHPT.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>25-OH vitamin D</strong></td><td>Find secondary stimulation and optimize bone health.</td><td>May be low in PHPT, but deficiency is not diagnostic.</td><td>Deficiency can raise PTH, lower urine calcium, and occasionally mask the degree of hypercalcemia.</td><td>Replete cautiously to >30 ng/mL and reassess when needed.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Creatinine / eGFR / CrCl</strong></td><td>Identify secondary HPT and renal surgical criteria.</td><td>PHPT can occur at any kidney function, but CrCl/eGFR <60 is an operative criterion.</td><td>CKD itself raises PTH and lowers urine calcium. eGFR is an estimate.</td><td>Use creatinine clearance when the distinction or trend matters.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>24-hour urine calcium</strong></td><td>Assess hypercalciuria, stone risk, and FHH probability.</td><td>Often normal-high or elevated; >250 mg/day in women or >300 mg/day in men is a surgical criterion in current workshop guidance.</td><td>Low result can reflect FHH, incomplete collection, thiazide/lithium, low intake, vitamin D deficiency, or CKD.</td><td>Assess collection creatinine and context; calculate CCCR when appropriate.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>CCCR</strong></td><td>Supports PHPT vs FHH.</td><td>>0.02 favors PHPT; <0.01 favors FHH.</td><td>There is substantial overlap. It is not a definitive test.</td><td>Use age, lifelong calcium pattern, family history, confounders, and genetic testing.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Alkaline phosphatase</strong></td><td>Estimate skeletal turnover and hungry-bone risk.</td><td>Normal or mildly elevated in mild disease; marked elevation suggests active bone disease.</td><td>Total ALP can be hepatic.</td><td>Clarify source and consider postoperative calcium risk if markedly elevated.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Magnesium</strong></td><td>Important in perioperative hypocalcemia.</td><td>Not diagnostic of PHPT.</td><td>Hypomagnesemia can impair PTH secretion/action and complicate calcium correction.</td><td>Check and replace when hypocalcemia occurs.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout"><strong>CCCR formula:</strong> (24-hour urine calcium × serum creatinine) ÷ (serum calcium × 24-hour urine creatinine). Use values from the same collection period and compatible units. A number is only useful if the collection and clinical context are credible.</div>
</section>

<section id="differential" class="searchable">
<h2>Differential diagnosis by pattern</h2>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Calcium</th><th>PTH</th><th>Phosphorus / urine</th><th>Clues</th><th>Decisive next step</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Primary HPT</strong></td><td>High</td><td>High or inappropriately normal</td><td>Phosphorus often low-normal; urine Ca variable</td><td>New/progressive hypercalcemia, stone or bone disease</td><td>Repeat biochemical confirmation, exclude FHH, stage target organs</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>FHH</strong></td><td>Mildly high, usually lifelong</td><td>Normal or mildly high</td><td>CCCR often <0.01</td><td>Young age, family history, stable lifelong calcium</td><td>Historic/family calcium and genetic testing when suspected</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Thiazide-associated</strong></td><td>High or high-normal</td><td>May be nonsuppressed</td><td>Urine calcium reduced</td><td>Medication timing</td><td>Review indication; repeat after safe withdrawal when clinically feasible</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Lithium-associated</strong></td><td>High</td><td>High/nonsuppressed</td><td>Urine calcium may be low</td><td>Psychiatric therapy, possible multigland disease</td><td>Coordinate medication decision; evaluate as PHPT physiology</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Tertiary HPT</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Markedly high</td><td>Often high phosphorus in advanced CKD</td><td>Long-standing secondary HPT, dialysis or transplant</td><td>Use renal history and longitudinal PTH/calcium course</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Secondary HPT</strong></td><td>Normal or low</td><td>High</td><td>Depends on cause</td><td>Vitamin D deficiency, CKD, low intake, malabsorption, antiresorptives</td><td>Treat cause and repeat PTH/calcium</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Malignancy/PTHrP</strong></td><td>High, often more acute/severe</td><td>Suppressed</td><td>Often low phosphorus with PTHrP</td><td>Cancer context, weight loss, rapid rise</td><td>PTHrP and malignancy-directed evaluation when indicated</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Granulomatous disease/lymphoma</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Suppressed</td><td>1,25-(OH)2D may be elevated</td><td>Sarcoid, TB, lymphoma context</td><td>1,25-(OH)2D and disease-directed workup</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Vitamin D intoxication</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Suppressed</td><td>25-OH D elevated</td><td>Supplement exposure</td><td>Measure 25-OH D and stop source</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Milk-alkali physiology</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Suppressed</td><td>Metabolic alkalosis, kidney injury</td><td>High calcium carbonate/alkali intake</td><td>Exposure history and acid-base/renal evaluation</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
</section>

<section id="fhh" class="searchable">
<h2>PHPT versus FHH: do not oversimplify this</h2>
<div class="grid2">
  <div class="card"><div class="label teal">Features favoring PHPT</div><ul><li>Calcium became abnormal later in life or is rising</li><li>Kidney stones, nephrocalcinosis, osteoporosis, or fracture</li><li>CCCR >0.02</li><li>No family pattern</li><li>Urine calcium not low after confounders are addressed</li></ul></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label warn">Features favoring FHH</div><ul><li>Mild hypercalcemia since youth</li><li>Multiple family members with hypercalcemia</li><li>CCCR <0.01</li><li>Few classic PHPT complications</li><li>CASR, GNA11, or AP2S1 pathway variant</li></ul></div>
</div>
<div class="callout red"><strong>The cutoff is not clean.</strong> Up to roughly 20% of PHPT can have CCCR <0.01, some FHH patients exceed 0.02, and many patients fall between 0.01 and 0.02. Thiazides, lithium, vitamin D deficiency, CKD, low intake, and an incomplete collection can falsely lower urine calcium.</div>
<div class="callout teal"><strong>Practical rule:</strong> when FHH remains plausible, do not let a single urine result decide surgery. Use historical calcium, family testing, collection quality, confounders, and genetics.</div>
</section>

<section id="normocalcemic" class="searchable">
<h2>Normocalcemic PHPT</h2>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>This is a diagnosis of exclusion.</strong> It should not be used as a quick label for osteoporosis plus a mildly elevated PTH.</div>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="step">Confirm <strong>normal albumin-adjusted total calcium and normal ionized calcium</strong>.</div>
  <div class="step">Confirm <strong>elevated PTH on at least two occasions over 3–6 months</strong>.</div>
  <div class="step">Exclude vitamin D insufficiency; aim for 25-OH vitamin D >30 ng/mL and allow time for PTH to normalize.</div>
  <div class="step">Exclude renal insufficiency, generally eGFR/CrCl <60 mL/min.</div>
  <div class="step">Assess inadequate calcium intake, malabsorption/bariatric surgery, hypercalciuria, phosphate disorders, and medications such as bisphosphonates, denosumab, lithium, diuretics, anticonvulsants, PPIs, and SGLT2 inhibitors when relevant.</div>
  <div class="step">Only after exclusions and persistence should normocalcemic PHPT be considered.</div>
</div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Surgery:</strong> current international workshop guidance does not provide a universal operative threshold for normocalcemic PHPT because evidence is limited. Management should be individualized, especially when there is progressive objective bone or renal disease.</div>
</section>

<section id="organ" class="searchable">
<h2>Assess target-organ involvement</h2>
<div class="grid2">
  <div class="card"><div class="label">Skeleton</div><ul><li>DXA at lumbar spine, hip, and distal one-third radius</li><li>Use T-score in postmenopausal women and men ≥50; use Z-score for younger patients as appropriate</li><li>VFA or vertebral X-ray when height loss, back pain, low BMD, glucocorticoids, or clinical concern</li><li>TBS can add information if available</li><li>Do not omit the radius: PHPT disproportionately affects cortical bone</li></ul></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label teal">Kidney</div><ul><li>Creatinine clearance or eGFR</li><li>Full 24-hour urine calcium</li><li>Renal ultrasound or noncontrast CT for stones/nephrocalcinosis</li><li>Stone-risk profile when stones or hypercalciuria are present</li><li>Do not rely on stone history alone; stones can be silent</li></ul></div>
</div>
<div class="callout teal"><strong>Important terminology:</strong> a patient discovered on screening can still have “asymptomatic PHPT with target-organ involvement.” Finding a silent stone or vertebral fracture changes management even if the patient had no complaint.</div>
</section>

<section id="surgery" class="searchable">
<h2>Surgery decision</h2>
<div class="callout green"><strong>Parathyroidectomy is the only curative treatment.</strong> All symptomatic patients should be offered surgery unless contraindicated. For asymptomatic disease, meeting any one criterion is enough.</div>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Current criterion</th><th>Clinical meaning</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Serum calcium</td><td>&gt;1.0 mg/dL above the laboratory ULN</td><td>Persistent biochemical severity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bone density</td><td>T-score ≤ −2.5 at lumbar spine, total hip, femoral neck, or distal one-third radius</td><td>Objective skeletal involvement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fracture</td><td>Vertebral fracture on VFA/X-ray or low-trauma fracture</td><td>Operate regardless of BMD value</td></tr>
<tr><td>Renal function</td><td>eGFR or creatinine clearance &lt;60 mL/min</td><td>Renal indication</td></tr>
<tr><td>Renal imaging</td><td>Nephrolithiasis or nephrocalcinosis</td><td>Silent or symptomatic disease qualifies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hypercalciuria</td><td>&gt;250 mg/day in women or &gt;300 mg/day in men</td><td>Revised lower thresholds in the Fifth Workshop</td></tr>
<tr><td>Age</td><td>&lt;50 years</td><td>Age alone is sufficient</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="grid2">
  <div class="card"><div class="label teal">Also refer for surgery</div><ul><li>Patient prefers definitive treatment</li><li>Surveillance is not feasible or desired</li><li>Suspected parathyroid carcinoma</li><li>Hypercalcemic crisis after stabilization</li><li>Progression during monitoring</li></ul></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label warn">Do not promise surgery will fix</div><ul><li>Nonspecific fatigue</li><li>Depression or cognitive complaints</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Cardiovascular risk</li></ul><p class="muted">These may improve in some patients, but evidence is not reliable enough to use as the sole expected outcome.</p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="localization" class="searchable">
<h2>Localization and operative planning</h2>
<div class="callout red"><strong>Negative imaging does not negate a biochemical diagnosis and does not automatically rule out surgery.</strong></div>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Modality</th><th>Use</th><th>Limitations</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Neck ultrasound</td><td>First-line localization; evaluates thyroid simultaneously</td><td>Operator dependent; ectopic glands and multigland disease are harder</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sestamibi ± SPECT/CT</td><td>Functional localization, especially single adenoma</td><td>Lower sensitivity in small glands, multigland disease, and some thyroid disease</td></tr>
<tr><td>4D-CT</td><td>Discordant/negative first-line imaging or reoperative setting</td><td>Radiation and iodinated contrast</td></tr>
<tr><td>18F-fluorocholine PET/CT</td><td>Selected difficult or reoperative cases</td><td>Availability, cost, radiation</td></tr>
<tr><td>MRI</td><td>Selected patients when radiation/contrast issues matter</td><td>Availability and variable sensitivity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Venous sampling</td><td>Rare, difficult reoperative cases</td><td>Invasive; expert-center test</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<ul><li>Use an experienced parathyroid surgeon.</li><li>Focused surgery is reasonable with convincing localization and appropriate intraoperative strategy.</li><li>Bilateral exploration remains effective, especially for suspected multigland disease or nonlocalizing imaging.</li><li>Parathyroid FNA is not routine; avoid it unless a specific expert-level diagnostic problem justifies it.</li></ul>
</section>

<section id="medical" class="searchable">
<h2>Nonoperative and bridging management</h2>
<p>Do not use “medical management” as a vague substitute for deciding what problem you are treating.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Intervention</th><th>Expected effect</th><th>Limitation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>General care</td><td>Hydration, mobility, avoid dehydration, review offending drugs</td><td>Reduces avoidable worsening of hypercalcemia</td><td>Not curative</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calcium intake</td><td>Maintain recommended nutritional intake; do not reflexively restrict</td><td>Avoids secondary PTH stimulation</td><td>Monitor if severe hypercalcemia or unusual intake</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vitamin D deficiency</td><td>Cautious cholecalciferol replacement</td><td>Goal generally >30 ng/mL and below lab upper limit; may lower PTH</td><td>Monitor serum and urine calcium when clinically relevant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hypercalcemia</td><td>Cinacalcet</td><td>Lowers serum calcium; may partially lower PTH</td><td>Does not reliably improve BMD</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low BMD/osteoporosis</td><td>Alendronate or denosumab when appropriate</td><td>Improves BMD</td><td>Does not cure PHPT and usually does not normalize calcium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Both high calcium and low BMD</td><td>Cinacalcet plus antiresorptive</td><td>Targets both problems</td><td>Still not definitive treatment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Severe acute hypercalcemia</td><td>Hospital-level IV hydration and rapid calcium-lowering therapy; definitive surgery after stabilization when feasible</td><td>Stabilizes organ dysfunction and symptoms</td><td>Requires individualized inpatient protocol</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout red"><strong>Wrong-target errors:</strong> cinacalcet is not an osteoporosis drug. Bisphosphonates and denosumab are not reliable calcium-normalizing treatments for chronic PHPT. Choose treatment based on the endpoint.</div>
</section>

<section id="monitoring" class="searchable">
<h2>Monitoring when surgery is deferred</h2>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Interval</th><th>Trigger to reconsider surgery</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Serum calcium</td><td>Annually; sooner if changing</td><td>Consistently >1 mg/dL above ULN or meaningful rise</td></tr>
<tr><td>25-OH vitamin D</td><td>Annually</td><td>Persistent deficiency or replacement-related change</td></tr>
<tr><td>Renal function</td><td>Annually; creatinine clearance preferred when precision matters</td><td>Significant decline or value <60 mL/min</td></tr>
<tr><td>PTH</td><td>As clinically indicated</td><td>Interpret with calcium; PTH alone is not the primary progression endpoint</td></tr>
<tr><td>Three-site DXA</td><td>Every 1–2 years; less often if robustly normal and stable</td><td>Significant loss or T-score ≤−2.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertebral imaging</td><td>When clinically indicated</td><td>New vertebral or low-trauma fracture</td></tr>
<tr><td>Renal imaging</td><td>When clinically indicated after baseline assessment</td><td>New stone or nephrocalcinosis</td></tr>
<tr><td>24-hour urine calcium</td><td>When clinically indicated</td><td>New hypercalciuria or stone-risk concern</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout teal"><strong>Monitoring is an active plan, not “follow up PRN.”</strong> Document which labs and imaging will be repeated, when, and which result will trigger surgery.</div>
</section>

<section id="postop" class="searchable">
<h2>Postoperative care and long-term follow-up</h2>
<h3>Immediate postoperative questions</h3>
<div class="grid2">
  <div class="card"><div class="label">Monitor</div><ul><li>Symptoms of hypocalcemia</li><li>Serum calcium; more than daily if hungry-bone risk is high</li><li>PTH when needed to clarify physiology</li><li>Phosphorus and magnesium</li><li>Neck hematoma, voice change, airway symptoms</li></ul></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="label warn">Higher hungry-bone risk</div><ul><li>Very high preoperative PTH</li><li>Markedly elevated ALP</li><li>Severe hypercalcemia</li><li>Osteitis fibrosa cystica, brown tumors, multiple fractures</li><li>Large adenoma burden</li><li>Low preoperative vitamin D</li></ul></div>
</div>
<h3>Low calcium after surgery: distinguish the mechanism</h3>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Postoperative hypoparathyroidism</th><th>Hungry bone syndrome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>PTH</td><td>Low or inappropriately normal</td><td>Normal or high</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phosphorus</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td></tr>
<tr><td>Magnesium</td><td>Variable</td><td>Often low</td></tr>
<tr><td>Urine calcium on replacement</td><td>Normal or high</td><td>Low</td></tr>
<tr><td>Timing/context</td><td>Immediately after loss or stunning of parathyroid tissue</td><td>Often becomes prominent around postoperative days 3–5 in high-turnover bone disease</td></tr>
<tr><td>Treatment principle</td><td>Calcium ± active vitamin D; monitor for recovery</td><td>Aggressive and sometimes prolonged calcium, active vitamin D, magnesium, and phosphate-aware management</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Define success correctly</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Biochemical cure:</strong> normocalcemia maintained for at least 6 months.</li><li><strong>Persistent PHPT:</strong> failure to achieve durable normocalcemia during the first 6 months.</li><li><strong>Recurrent PHPT:</strong> hypercalcemia returns after at least 6 months of documented normocalcemia.</li><li><strong>Normal calcium with elevated PTH is not automatically recurrence.</strong> Check vitamin D, renal function, calcium intake, malabsorption, medications, and skeletal remineralization.</li><li>After successful surgery, check calcium at 6 months to document cure and then at least annually; measure PTH if hypercalcemia recurs or another clinical question exists.</li></ul>
</section>

<section id="special" class="searchable">
<h2>Special situations</h2>
<details><summary>Pregnancy</summary><div><p>Use coordinated endocrine, endocrine-surgery, and maternal-fetal medicine care. Maintain hydration and monitor calcium in mild disease. Avoid bisphosphonates and denosumab. When surgery is needed, the second trimester is generally preferred; ultrasound is the localization modality of choice. If surgery is deferred, monitor the neonate for hypocalcemia.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Hypercalcemic crisis</summary><div><p>This is inpatient disease. Treat dehydration and organ dysfunction immediately, use rapid calcium-lowering therapy according to renal function and institutional protocol, and prioritize definitive parathyroidectomy after stabilization when feasible.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Possible parathyroid carcinoma</summary><div><p>Red flags include markedly high calcium/PTH, severe renal and skeletal disease, a palpable neck mass, vocal-cord dysfunction, or an invasive/adherent lesion. Avoid biopsy. Refer to an expert endocrine surgeon because the initial en bloc operation offers the best chance of durable control.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Young patient, multigland disease, or family history</summary><div><p>Consider MEN1, MEN2A, MEN4, CDC73-related hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumor syndrome, familial isolated hyperparathyroidism, and FHH. Genetic evaluation is particularly appropriate in patients under 30, multigland disease, family hypercalcemia, syndromic findings, atypical adenoma, or carcinoma.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Persistent or recurrent disease</summary><div><p>First reconfirm the biochemical diagnosis and exclude FHH or secondary causes. Review the operative report, pathology, intraoperative PTH, and all prior imaging. Reoperative localization may use 4D-CT or fluorocholine PET/CT in expert centers. Reoperation carries higher nerve and hypoparathyroidism risk and should be done by a high-volume reoperative parathyroid surgeon.</p></div></details>
</section>

<section id="cases" class="searchable">
<h2>Case walkthroughs</h2>
<details open><summary>Case 1: Calcium 10.8, albumin 4.0, PTH 58</summary><div><p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> calcium is truly elevated. A PTH of 58 may be inside the laboratory range, but it is not suppressed; therefore the process is PTH-dependent. PHPT is most likely, but review thiazide/lithium and evaluate FHH. Repeat calcium/PTH on a second occasion, obtain 25-OH D, renal function, 24-hour urine calcium/creatinine, DXA including radius, and renal imaging.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Case 2: Calcium 9.6, PTH 105, vitamin D 14, eGFR 82</summary><div><p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> do not diagnose normocalcemic PHPT. Vitamin D deficiency is an obvious secondary driver. Replete vitamin D and confirm adequate calcium intake, then repeat calcium, ionized calcium, and PTH after an appropriate interval.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Case 3: Calcium 10.7 since age 20, PTH 54, CCCR 0.006</summary><div><p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> FHH is a major concern. Confirm the collection and confounders, review family calcium values, and pursue genetic evaluation when appropriate. Do not send directly to parathyroidectomy.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Case 4: Calcium 12.4, PTH 7</summary><div><p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> PTH is suppressed. This is not a standard PHPT workup. Evaluate the tempo and context; consider PTHrP-mediated malignancy, bone destruction, vitamin D-mediated disease, supplements/medications, and other PTH-independent causes.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Case 5: Dialysis patient, PTH 1400, calcium 11.2, phosphorus 6.8</summary><div><p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> the renal history, very high PTH, hypercalcemia, and hyperphosphatemia suggest tertiary hyperparathyroidism rather than sporadic PHPT. Management follows CKD-mineral bone disease and renal hyperparathyroidism pathways.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Case 6: Confirmed PHPT, calcium 10.6, age 64, T-score −2.7 at distal radius</summary><div><p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> the patient meets a surgical criterion based on BMD even though calcium is only mildly elevated and symptoms may be absent. Refer to an experienced parathyroid surgeon after completing renal evaluation and localization planning.</p></div></details>
</section>

<section id="pitfalls" class="searchable">
<h2>High-value pitfalls</h2>
<div class="card"><ol>
<li><strong>Calling a normal PTH “normal” during hypercalcemia.</strong> It should be suppressed.</li>
<li><strong>Ordering localization imaging before confirming the diagnosis.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Using a single calcium/PTH pair to make a lifelong diagnosis.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Diagnosing normocalcemic PHPT before correcting vitamin D, calcium intake, renal disease, or medication effects.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Using CCCR as a binary truth test.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Forgetting that thiazides, lithium, CKD, vitamin D deficiency, and incomplete urine collection lower urinary calcium.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Omitting the distal one-third radius from DXA.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Assuming “asymptomatic” means no renal or skeletal disease.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Using the obsolete >400 mg/day urine-calcium surgical threshold.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Using cinacalcet for the wrong target.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Calling isolated elevated PTH after surgery recurrent PHPT despite normal calcium.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Sending a possible reoperative case to a low-volume surgeon.</strong></li>
</ol></div>
</section>

<section id="references" class="searchable">
<h2>Guideline basis and scope</h2>
<div class="callout"><strong>Clinical basis:</strong> the core diagnostic, evaluation, operative, monitoring, nutritional, and medication logic is aligned primarily with the Fifth International Workshop on PHPT (2022), with operative and postoperative concepts cross-checked against endocrine-surgery and European expert guidance. The page is written independently and does not reproduce a textbook chapter.</div>
<div class="source">
<strong>Key sources:</strong>
<ol>
<li>Bilezikian JP, et al. Evaluation and Management of Primary Hyperparathyroidism: Summary Statement and Guidelines from the Fifth International Workshop. <em>J Bone Miner Res.</em> 2022;37:2293–2314.</li>
<li>Wilhelm SM, et al. The American Association of Endocrine Surgeons Guidelines for Definitive Management of Primary Hyperparathyroidism. <em>JAMA Surg.</em> 2016;151:959–968.</li>
<li>Bollerslev J, et al. European expert consensus on practical management of specific aspects of parathyroid disorders in adults and in pregnancy. <em>Eur J Endocrinol.</em> 2022;186:R33–R63.</li>
<li>Perrier ND, et al. Surgical Aspects of Primary Hyperparathyroidism. <em>J Bone Miner Res.</em> 2022;37:2373–2390.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is an educational workflow for clinician review. It is not an EHR-integrated decision support system, does not account for every assay or comorbidity, and should not be used as the sole basis for patient care.</p>
</div>
</section>
